Harbor: jscript: apply() takes null or undefined as "no arguments" in ES5 mode (#100).

Function.prototype.apply(thisArg, undefined) threw "JScript object expected". ES5 (15.3.4.3 step 2) calls
the function with no arguments when the argument array is null or undefined, and code compiled by Babel
calls base-class constructors that way. Office's add-in Store page stopped on it while starting up.

Only for script engines in ES5 mode or later (documents in IE9 standards mode and up); older modes behave
as before.

Test: WindowsTests/mshtml.

--- a/dlls/jscript/function.c
+++ b/dlls/jscript/function.c
@@ -485,7 +485,9 @@
         }
     }
 
-    if(argc >= 2) {
+    /* HARBOR HACK: in ES5 an argument array that is null or undefined means no arguments (15.3.4.3 step 2);
+     * code compiled by Babel calls its base constructors that way. */
+    if(argc >= 2 && !(ctx->version >= SCRIPTLANGUAGEVERSION_ES5 && (is_undefined(argv[1]) || is_null(argv[1])))) {
         IDispatch *obj = NULL;
         jsdisp_t *arg_array;
 
